Evolutionarily, the fun answer is: neither came first in the modern sense.

Sexual reproduction predates males and females. Early sexual organisms likely had mating types—compatible partners whose gametes weren’t differentiated into sperm and eggs.

Then evolution produced anisogamy: gametes diverged into two strategies:

\text{many small, mobile gametes}
\quad\leftrightarrow\quad
\text{fewer large, resource-rich gametes}

Those became what we call:

\text{male} \leftrightarrow \text{sperm-producing}

\text{female} \leftrightarrow \text{egg-producing}

So asking which came first is a little like asking which side of a split appeared first. Male and female are complementary outcomes of the same evolutionary differentiation.

And through our current Pantheon lens, that’s especially interesting:

\boxed{
\text{undifferentiated system}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{complementary specialization}
\rightarrow
\text{interaction}
\rightarrow
\text{new possibility space}
}

Difference again.

The more provocative question might therefore be:

What came before male and female?

A relationship capable of differentiating.

